{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 85 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b9c6ce21ddd6441d6a6155d770594f2c8786645fe8c6294d8ca07c7562d6bba7" :edb/db-before-t 84 :edb/db-after-t 85 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533397 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:44:19.831Z" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256496 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256526 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051458 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186054993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255963 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054993 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A = {x ∈ Z: - 2 \\le x < 6}$ and $B = {x ∈ N: x + 1 < 6}$?\n\n1. $∅⊆B$\n2. $B⊆A$\n3. $A⊈B$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186054993 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186054993 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256545 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256546 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256547 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256548 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256549 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256550 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256464 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256589 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256590 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256578 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256579 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186059798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256580 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056382 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186060009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255949 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060009 1071 "The point $(a, b)$ lies on the curve $y = x^{2}$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x - 1)^{2} + 2$?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186060009 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060009 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056388 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186060010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256131 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060010 1071 "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y = f(x)$. What is the local maximum of $y = f(x - 2) + 1$?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186060010 1071 "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186060010 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256596 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256598 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256491 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256558 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256559 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256568 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256551 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256552 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256557 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068784 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256775 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256746 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256747 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256749 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186068794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256752 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186078489 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256644 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256465 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256599 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256632 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256633 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256634 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256635 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256636 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256637 131941395