94139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256776 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256777 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256778 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089291 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255953 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092525 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2), (-1, 2), (1, 1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{- π}$ by an angle of $π$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(-1,-1)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092525 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2),(-1,2),(1,1).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,-1)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092525 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,2),(1,-2),(-1,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089297 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256342 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092526 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-3),(-3,2),(2,4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(-4,2)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092526 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3), (-3, 2), (2, 4)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{-90^{∘}}$ by an angle of $90^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n1. $(-3, 2)$\n2. $(4,-2)$\n3. $(-4, 2)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092526 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,2),(2,3),(4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089303 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256099 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092527 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1), (1, 4), (1,-1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $R_{π/2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{π}{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(4,-2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(1, 1)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186092527 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(1,4),(1,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092527 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-4,1),(1,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186092534 1068 #edb/ref 175921860