i\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=-i+j.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168537 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186172284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256066 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172284 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $G(x, y) = x^{2}i - y^{2}j$\n2. $H(x, y) = \\ln yi - e^{xy}j$\n3. $K(x, y, z) = xi - yj + zk$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186172284 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172284 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168543 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186172285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256097 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172285 1071 "Which of 