ector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=-k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,k.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168579 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186172291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256082 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172291 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $U(x, y, z) = ⟨x + 2, \\sqrt{x - y}, \\ln z⟩$\n2. $V(x, y) = ⟨xe^{y}, 1 - y⟩$\n3. $W(t) = ⟨t^{2}, 2t, 2 - 3t⟩$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186172291 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186172291 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ 