592186179722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256786 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256787 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256788 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256645 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256674 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256676 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256677 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256681 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179724 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256673 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179724 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256680 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256703 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256707 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256711 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256714 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256765 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256766 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256767 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256768 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256771 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256772 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256773 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256774 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256783 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256754 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256755 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256756 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256759 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256735 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177345 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256007 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179951 1071 "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x^{2}(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) < 0$?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179951 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179951 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aecfe6b9374576ae002e439ff756d41ba29eb87d2f99cf3569dd3332e0128b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/ee/eedbe9fe606042eb34d0067132e5bfccfea6e6528e3eaf7091f0c237a5fe6ca3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c800752188e6dd292d47f7153462077288540dd232f76a03472b2bab3d20c0.png)" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177417 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255980 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179963 1071 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179963 1071 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179963 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡t-16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =32{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =32(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177447 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256095 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179968 1071 "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\frac{4y}{x}ds$, where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse $x^{2} + 16y^{2} = 16$, shown above?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179968 1071 "![](images/3b/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179968 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177453 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256086 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179969 1071 "Evaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\frac{24x}{y}ds$,\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} = 8x$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8}, 3)$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179969 1071 "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179969 1074 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177465 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256052 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179971 1071 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y, z) = x\\ln (z - 2y)$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179971 1071 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179971 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177483 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256136 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179974 1071 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the quarter-circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$, traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4, 0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179974 1071 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179974 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0baaafbe9846ccef2c06a3b1a86eccac394344afc2dfd94bba2907ebb1cf2362.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177501 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256034 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179977 1071 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179977 1071 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179977 1074 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177519 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255960 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179980 1071 "Let $P = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[0, 3]$. If $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$, calculate $∑_(i = 1)^(m)5Δx_{i}$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179980 1071 "Let $P={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,3].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179980 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5Δ{x}_{i}=5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[0,3],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=5\\cdot 3=15.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177525 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256339 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179981 1071 "Let $P = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[0, 2]$. If $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$, calculate $∑_(i = 1)^(m)(x_{i} + x_{i - 1})Δx_{i}$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179981 1071 "Let $P={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,2].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179981 1074 "We can simplify the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${x}_{0}=0$ and ${x}_{m}=2$ are the endpoints of our partition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177549 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255969 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179985 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0, 2]$\n- $P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[1, 6]$\n\nCalculate $∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$, where $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$." 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179985 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,6]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179985 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,2]$ and $[1,6]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-0=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =6-1=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 5=10.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177561 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256145 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179987 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y$, traversed along the line segment from $(0, 2)$ to $(3, 1)$?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 false] [17592186179987 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186179987 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-