 true] [17592186256374 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256374 1077 "selection" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256374 1079 #edb/ref 17592186254726 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256374 1080 #uuid "ff28f4c7-b721-56cb-a6ef-a2b3a670e104" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254715 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255176 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255687 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255715 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255825 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1077 "selection" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1079 #edb/ref 17592186255176 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256375 1080 #uuid "ffffbcf7-2290-5771-97a7-2e3fca38c6aa" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255973 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1069 "q-120244" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1071 "![](images/9e/9e595809380e3067934b9f8c86933dd5300fcfb54ce4202eddc0e4196b74cf00.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1072 #uuid "701d4729-670a-58df-8c65-907d28621390" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256376 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c785e189beebb0a803bceba272db156af6d3b47c690ee9527876f77c65d1891.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256138 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1069 "q-120245" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1071 "![](images/58/58901401bfe81e040e22da6e07d543423db58a845009a80525eae131752874d4.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1072 #uuid "5bdd11f1-936c-5b1f-8197-4ca4897841c7" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256377 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3e/3effb654f4db5e1f207c671116baf648cb70f90781882a1fd758f32b610aefca.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=2a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256058 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1069 "q-120253" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1071 "The growth rate in the number $P(t)$ of files infected with a virus in a computer is jointly proportional to the number of infected files at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the difference between the computer's carrying capacity and the number of infected files. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $100\\,000.$ How many files will have the virus after $12$ hours given that there were $1000$ files with the virus initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1072 #uuid "f1f51ad0-9c2b-57e3-b330-0f251db3c8fa" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256378 1074 "Since the number of files infected with the virus' growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of infected files at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of infected files, the number of files infected with the virus follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100\\,000.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of files infected with the virus is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(100\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1000 \\\\ \\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5(0)}} & =1000 \\\\ 1+C & =100 \\\\ C & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=12$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 12}} \\\\  & ≈80\\,296.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256206 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1069 "q-120264" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1071 "![](images/f4/f4a6aa53604ce4381a6627097ba4ebc5853a67ff9fa1675b98e5114775cefdc6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=4-y$\n- ${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1072 #uuid "b482f1cd-a6f6-5695-b088-921969df414d" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256379 1074 "![](images/7d/7d0d7ff6fa9c7cd074fcc2321f125ac5da8142e55578459f44903555e2797204.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y=4$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y-2xy=2y(4-x)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y=(x-4)^{2}y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256116 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1069 "q-120266" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1071 "![](images/26/26a0a41cc50b6d5cb586c52e6a4e6bd5bd324f3e13ad7eef4ee0d9eed18340ea.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1072 #uuid "3c9d7302-1f93-5f85-9a08-01c47b45f471" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256380 1074 "![](images/24/249aba327a8103645963ce95e7935019a279b4cf0d9306111c41172ae22f792b.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy=(2-x)y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=(x+2)^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=(x-2){y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255943 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1069 "q-120268" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1071 "![](images/25/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1072 #uuid "cf2e5cbf-66fb-56e7-95be-719efd096999" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256381 1074 "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256235 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1069 "q-120271" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1071 "![](images/85/85fcea0ad26f7da26189c7611a851bfe228d3ad5d428dcba6c8299c8304540d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1072 #uuid "01d08d57-0bae-5647-8907-efecd1b71deb" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256382 1074 "![](images/dc/dc75845932358d060d5878a30f00fa01e2b7b54408bca308110a53ea654ae18c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1=(x-1)(y+1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)=(x-1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)=(1-x)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256093 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1069 "q-120277" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1071 "![](images/c9/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1072 #uuid "15e9fa4b-b469-5d54-bb3e-fd8c74e844aa" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256383 1074 "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & 