186045585 true] [17592186256385 1072 #uuid "b4d0ef3c-f0b3-5fa4-b071-4e8740b424bd" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256385 1074 "![](images/cc/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256184 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1069 "q-120282" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1071 "![](images/e9/e90d9a160db2c75091253eaa8a8c815e92c45f988841683f5a4d18ca6a6ea7db.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1072 #uuid "69c58940-535c-5bff-aff2-b382e99c5568" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256386 1074 "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256242 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1069 "q-120285" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1071 "![](images/c4/c4d26b3db6446f586ca47634d9f9a3a004968a0081b8859ce46b4b4af799d066.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=3:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1072 #uuid "eee74ea9-5018-5a1b-8460-e61ca7767d79" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256387 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=3:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{3}{10}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{3}{10}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{3}{10}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256111 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1069 "q-120286" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1071 "![](images/e8/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1072 #uuid "a8520070-e078-5b91-88f5-b7a922021b9b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256388 1074 "![](images/2f/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256028 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1069 "q-120294" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1071 "![](images/70/707beca1d856414e25874cc0b56c6654fbb41d699f01106b6749d49408b39aee.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=7:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1072 #uuid "e34b700c-a9ab-5795-931b-c570187a69f9" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256389 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=7:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{7}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{7}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256301 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1069 "q-120295" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1072 #uuid "f6623b3b-0ac5-59c1-a0a1-f8ed0a13b015" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256390 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(3,0).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255936 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1069 "q-120298" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=6{x}^{2}$ on the interval $[-1,2].$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1072 #uuid "83d4bc37-f08b-5807-b19f-7111b64248d0" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256391 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-(-1)}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (2{x}^{3})|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2(2)^{3}-2(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =6 \\\\ 6{c}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =1 \\\\ c & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256029 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1069 "q-120303" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1071 "![](images/50/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=n$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1072 #uuid "c29ac42f-42ba-5668-9b76-4de9b80fc725" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256392 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-m+n \\\\  & =n-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(n-m).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =m+\\frac{2}{3}(n-m) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256096 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1069 "q-120311" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1071 "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1072 #uuid "cffd31fb-6e24-58d9-bedb-a4c483fcb431" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256393 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256155 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1069 "q-120321" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1071 "Simplify $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1072 #uuid "ee9051d9-6833-5d1b-9a2b-b13b007cd1ba" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256394 1074 "First, let's recall the identity\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression and using the fact that $\\frac{1}{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }={cot}^{3}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta } & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{3}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256245 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1069 "q-120328" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1071 "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1072 #uuid "3fe83e25-a491-5f7c-b624-e2aecbbcfc65" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256395 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256163 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1069 "q-12033" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(3,3),$ and $(5,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(5,-2)$\n- $(-2,-5)$\n- $(3,12)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1072 #uuid "7fe1aa4a-edf0-5fec-a75f-5eaee41d4322" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256396 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-5),(3,12),(5,13).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255996 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1069 "q-120333" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=5\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1072 #uuid "8d60eb92-3872-5934-a417-630e1b43c358" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256397 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{5/2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\cdot {2}^{5/2}-2\\cdot {0}^{5/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{{c}^{3}} & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{32}{25}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255982 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1069 "q-120347" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1071 "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1072 #uuid "6bb4ecb6-0b76-51e4-aa33-5be6f12e1dbb" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256398 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256181 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1069 "q-120348" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1071 "Express $\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1072 #uuid "cf1faff6-afe0-59ea-a58f-f8289c637840" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256399 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={35}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {35}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256225 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1069 "q-120352" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1071 "Express $\\frac{5}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-\\frac{5}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1072 #uuid "a4d26858-a337-5218-af76-0e3d4aac3fca" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256400 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}({cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={38}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {38}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{76}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}({cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{76}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256023 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1069 "q-120353" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1071 "Express $5{cos}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}-5{sin}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1072 #uuid "930ea824-2f2e-539a-bad4-2c6ed24d98e2" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256401 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n5({cos}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={16}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {16}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({cos}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{16}^{∘}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256361 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1069 "q-120357" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1071 "Express $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({15}^{∘})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1072 #uuid "c6b8a43b-52ca-5dc3-8609-7ee28897f062" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256402 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={15}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256030 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1069 "q-120358" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1071 "Express $3-6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1072 #uuid "6564bb9b-39cf-5d47-ba17-b3f0f77c4c7a" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256403 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255983 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1069 "q-120362" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1071 "Express $\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1072 #uuid "de163629-b9d5-5fdd-bc23-30faf9d38fa4" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256404 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={55}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {55}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256017 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1069 "q-120367" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1071 "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1072 #uuid "908ae38c-0e5f-5da6-8253-8eb427ca6c40" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256405 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256287 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1069 "q-120368" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1071 "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡{165}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{165}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1072 #uuid "2db81725-7807-50fc-8c34-2ee3aee5909f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256406 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{165}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{165}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{330}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256032 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1069 "q-120369" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1071 "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1072 #uuid "f9ed8218-8b72-5f11-b5f2-bf16df5e7136" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256407 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256280 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1069 "q-120370" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1071 "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1072 #uuid "1ee8111c-4b37-517d-b1b7-ddd1df3f014f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256408 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={157.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({157.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {157.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{315}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256063 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1069 "q-120380" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1071 "Given that $P(A)=0.5,$ $P(B)=0.3,$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.7,$ find $P({A}^{′}\\cup B).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1072 #uuid "8f5e21ac-677f-5190-a625-dd37da09f7a6" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256409 1074 "First, we calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.7 & =0.5+0.3-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.5+0.3-0.7 \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the corresponding Venn diagram and shade the region ${A}^{′}\\cup B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e573701578699d9c0fa5c0aa8f97d2c20fc916e867d0c89d3fe1256feccf69.png)\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{′}\\cup B)=0.1+0.2+0.3=0.6.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256001 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1069 "q-12040" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1072 #uuid "8408d555-50d3-5d93-86ae-518dfac40101" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256410 1074 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=5.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $5.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256197 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1069 "q-12041" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1072 #uuid "179b0a83-f4d2-55fc-982f-7126c97476ee" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256411 1074 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256075 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1069 "q-12042" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1072 #uuid "6c5fa46e-76dc-52b3-8ecb-22249e7437d5" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256412 1074 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=4i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $4.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256334 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1069 "q-120455" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1072 #uuid "b0e5e2dd-de81-57dc-8848-06ae6eac4cea" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256413 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256146 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1069 "q-120456" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1072 #uuid "ff4d5343-cd4d-538f-a96e-adc01ab24678" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256414 1074 "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](images/f6/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256175 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1069 "q-120468" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1072 #uuid "6d3f790c-6d1a-5380-94bb-56056dda53c1" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256415 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256341 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1069 "q-120476" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1072 #uuid "735542a6-3adb-5150-94ab-7fbabf60bc01" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256416 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256014 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1069 "q-120478" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1072 #uuid "43ac2ee7-84c7-5852-80b5-9c0dac95947e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256417 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255958 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1069 "q-120481" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$$$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1072 #uuid "1cb406ff-0039-5c3d-ab37-8667de0104d8" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256418 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256210 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1069 "q-120487" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-7=3+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+3=1$ $$\n- $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-5=-6$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1072 #uuid "2e821a2c-70b6-5ded-9b65-8ac018a20911" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256419 1074 "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256113 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1069 "q-120513" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1071 "Given $P(A)=\\frac{3}{8},$ $P(B)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{17}{24},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1072 #uuid "c8e55364-8c6b-5e2e-a3ec-b63e25a0bfdc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256420 1074 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{17}{24} & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{17}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+8-17}{24} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256062 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1069 "q-120524" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1071 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{8}{21}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(A).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1072 #uuid "98f88733-f310-5d24-9060-95462d921509" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256421 1074 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{8}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{63-32}{84} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{84}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256045 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1069 "q-120526" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1071 "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1072 #uuid "97dee9a3-7110-5d55-99c4-fd846fb1a671" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256422 1074 "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256358 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1069 "q-120543" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1071 "Given that $w=⟨-1,0,3⟩$, find $|\\,w\\,|.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1072 #uuid "10010191-d9e5-5c11-888d-317965c9e088" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256423 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256279 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1069 "q-120545" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1071 "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1072 #uuid "ef44a98c-d5e4-53f7-ad1a-45b75b68cb54" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256424 1074 "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256312 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1069 "q-120546" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1071 "Let $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(0,1,1).$ Find $|a+2b|,$ where $a$ and $b$ are position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1072 #uuid "57362383-d37c-5208-8fb8-78ed26f04bbc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256425 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i-k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $a+2b$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b & =(i-k)+2(j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+2b| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256195 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1069 "q-120548" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1072 #uuid "7c0bd99b-6e40-58e5-9873-3f39651f2371" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256426 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=(19.471\\,2…)^{∘}≈{19.471}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${19.471}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={19.471}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={19.471}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={19.471}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/05/05f0e5d6558c90bf467284d6b0ef17e7cfb546873bcf978e3d0cd8872c1f4436.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{19.471}^{∘} \\\\  & ={160.529}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈19.471\\cdot 160.529=3\\,126\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256208 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1069 "q-120554" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{8}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1072 #uuid "c30ff67b-fcaa-5191-a317-8a809ace1593" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256427 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=(-38.682\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{38.682}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{38.682}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={321.318}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={321.318}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4ddf01db733d5ee2e73a4b8abb0bec84d9ee2f7be0fea03e99c112456be1c14.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{38.682}^{∘} \\\\  & ={218.682}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=321.318\\cdot 218.682≈70\\,266\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256317 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1069 "q-120556" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1072 #uuid "18ab8ec6-b553-56a9-a2fc-b97b7ef3fec1" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256428 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4+2\\pi =6.115\\,785…≈6.115\\,8\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](images/f6/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6.115\\,8\\cdot 3.309\\,0=20.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256094 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1069 "q-120593" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1071 "![](images/48/48517c5d3fa340bed1cc7b5493f0099a72afa79d9e0690b79695afe018c8a05c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1072 #uuid "ec6a7f4f-9f9a-5a0c-bdce-ef6083c2204a" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256429 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$ \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256315 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1069 "q-120595" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1071 "![](images/5f/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1072 #uuid "0eb03380-bb2e-5373-833c-3b08aac5da8c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256430 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256336 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1069 "q-120597" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1071 "![](images/fd/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1072 #uuid "4cadc848-bed5-5538-a431-ac83515fffa4" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256431 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256065 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1069 "q-120598" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1071 "![](images/47/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1072 #uuid "1f77f386-bd94-517d-b58d-73799834bcb9" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256432 1074 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256134 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1069 "q-120608" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1072 #uuid "054ed097-32f2-5e6d-8799-43d62b3579f5" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256433 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256362 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1069 "q-120609" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-3$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1072 #uuid "c8c75db8-8a43-512c-9169-0fbba8f0ad8b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256434 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256109 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1069 "q-120616" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1072 #uuid "a6913064-54a5-5135-92c6-c2e39fa7af58" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256435 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/36/36b58603bada0da7822f8655646facce02cc63c08336b53945fe7f342da39732.png)" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256218 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1069 "q-120617" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1072 #uuid "a696b983-28a5-5461-b921-76324c38545f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256436 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/73/733f3a2a05b38ce040c5485e48f37082059283c2c7e92c2c8b16c3f89cabf632.png)" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256316 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1069 "q-120620" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1072 #uuid "8f20dda6-3a55-548a-ac2e-a4d15dbbe959" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256437 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256076 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1069 "q-120626" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1072 #uuid "168b3003-ea4a-5b92-b974-1dea82577827" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256438 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(-\\frac{3}{2})=-(56.309\\,9…)^{∘}≈-{56.310}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{56.310}^{∘}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{56.310}^{∘}={123.690}^{∘},\\,\\,{x}_{2}={360}^{∘}+x={360}^{∘}-{56.310}^{∘}={303.690}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n123.690\\cdot 303.690≈37\\,563\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256110 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1069 "q-120630" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1)=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1072 #uuid "af693eb4-5360-5ef3-9011-7331f6a3311c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256439 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1) & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+4 & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=-0.244\\,978≈-0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $-0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -0.245\\,0≈2.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-0.245\\,0,2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n(-0.245\\,0)\\cdot 2.896\\,6≈-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256057 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1069 "q-120631" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1071 "$\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}=$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1072 #uuid "93375bd6-9a3b-584f-abb5-493a4e9eddf0" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256440 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{({sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256101 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1069 "q-120632" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{23\\pi }{18}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1072 #uuid "ff8eef9b-8ba3-5269-9993-724992676dac" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256441 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{23\\pi }{18}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{23\\pi }{18}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is  \n\n$$\n\\frac{23\\pi }{18}-\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{18}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eadf7386a731c02931fc79d16d28d788c53917aa363100b32b4b2e9ec68db123.png)" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255994 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1069 "q-120635" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3-4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1072 #uuid "d6ff29e5-2a44-5e92-bb27-e38c871a4f7c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1073 true 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256442 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{2}{3})=(33.690\\,0…)^{∘}≈{33.690}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${33.690}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={33.690}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+{33.690}^{∘}=-{146.310}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{146.310}^{∘},\\,{33.690}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n(-146.310)\\cdot 33.690≈-4\\,929\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256165 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1069 "q-120637" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1071 "${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1072 #uuid "7483b5c0-3dc3-538f-b1c8-e2da92110f3a" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256443 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256321 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1069 "q-120639" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1071 "$\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1072 #uuid "c121a545-3cc0-5d24-b274-39c1f2ae43da" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256444 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256041 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1069 "q-120643" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3=3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1072 #uuid "0d475725-8f69-54aa-b65a-4f383452ac62" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256445 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256002 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1069 "q-120644" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1071 "$\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1072 #uuid "a0185c52-5699-5bfa-957f-ad7bf856cfa1" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256446 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256214 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1069 "q-120651" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1071 "$\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1072 #uuid "073899cb-a0ec-51ac-92ec-faf9fd7d512e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256447 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255955 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1069 "q-120652" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1071 "$\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1072 #uuid "c59c3ea2-945d-5573-80ad-ccc1ae2f546f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256448 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256005 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1069 "q-120653" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1071 "![](images/d5/d52d7a1e64de7618d5c9253ee00eafc43462e8a40e52fa7da671ef1f5a094e68.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1072 #uuid "89d9dad2-f14d-5fad-9b71-437bca49ffe5" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256449 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{8\\pi }{15}=\\frac{7\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256162 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [175