\\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256174 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1069 "q-120657" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1072 #uuid "c1861048-306a-5c9d-bc3a-136c2abd3f1d" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256451 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256079 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1069 "q-120660" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1072 #uuid "236e7298-9f51-5ebd-93c2-69c07e82f7f8" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256452 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1 & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}}-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}-1=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256115 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1069 "q-120661" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1072 #uuid "bba4c94f-f8c9-5ab5-a2b2-4d71544990cc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256453 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256257 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1069 "q-120665" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1071 "![](images/4e/4ec06d44ebac8021256a7ae130a55eb0972c70c5e01f26f6425143e3896eb441.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1072 #uuid "22fda7ab-d457-523c-8cd1-251f38cb936c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256454 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256351 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1069 "q-120666" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1072 #uuid "b8a55c6e-077f-5804-ac89-ec1be2eb0726" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256455 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression and use again $(2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)-1 \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256166 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1069 "q-120667" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1072 #uuid "ba60b74f-b26e-513b-8666-50189177373f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256456 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{4}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256329 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1069 "q-120701" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1071 "![](images/41/410822e9d39446151be39bc84028ef969e08eb967ece74f6c7e9fa0808ad3b76.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-1,\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1072 #uuid "72f73632-e278-546f-85e0-4f40f8dbbb76" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256457 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the second quadrant. So, we find the angle in the second quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256137 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1069 "q-120704" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1071 "![](images/03/03814c00b9e29f8a05507616ed08e3fe3daa484336e461012dc6c80799efc9e0.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{6},-\\sqrt{2})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1072 #uuid "6e7c36f9-b648-5bd3-b641-575b95a2bb92" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256458 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{6}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256140 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1069 "q-120708" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1071 "![](images/c3/c3d93c8d7d04af6783ef9149e15b5c1a59d1abb2744459004619a5ea2ef029e7.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,5)$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1072 #uuid "9ce22da1-26fe-5f91-a6ca-3a7a3af85710" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256459 1074 "The angles that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, its polar equation is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}).\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256219 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1069 "q-120712" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1071 "![](images/2d/2da1a7590ff9c8d403c040680c89f9bf310030d31399be7bd37209ef745a5bbd.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,-4)$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1072 #uuid "c196242b-12de-5dd4-9015-feb24f89fd6a" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256460 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-4}{4}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256085 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1069 "q-120719" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1071 "![](images/3b/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1072 #uuid "cae813d1-464d-5870-965e-a7cef5a78bcf" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256461 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256350 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1069 "q-120725" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1071 "![](images/d8/d8bbcd878edaef5d3f5b92d106a071444f20c72f3d419689d15644a4842a2d45.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1072 #uuid "2a3d0adf-9db5-5f2f-961d-9ca6ad8715dc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256462 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{3},-3).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256070 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1069 "q-120730" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1071 "![](images/84/84b17449880281367f50da89c39a7bcb1348c3a589aec74d9dec24eadb614ce7.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1072 #uuid "834a1b13-c35f-5b17-9f85-5c501c941a9d" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256463 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(8,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255991 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1069 "q-120733" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1071 "Let $a=⟨1,-2,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-3,-2⟩.$ Find $3a+2b.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1072 #uuid "78c0ef99-72a0-561a-bb7e-9a9c47519a2b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256464 1074 "To compute $3a+2b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+2b & =3\\cdot ⟨1,-2,0⟩+2\\cdot ⟨1,-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,0⟩+⟨2,-6,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-12,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256189 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1069 "q-120752" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1].$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1072 #uuid "38924e39-789b-5c5e-b0d2-35b2ccc7c0f1" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256465 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x){|}_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255938 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1069 "q-120755" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1072 #uuid "b95b86dd-f94a-5d2b-b1ff-4389482cc42c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256466 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(6)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =(6)^{2}-5(6)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t+4=(t-1)(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/ad/ad76f817f7c6e4a254e8881c34270eb875e72d821531566cb141cb393dcead72.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<4.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256167 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1069 "q-120757" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1072 #uuid "4aa695f4-838a-57f2-9880-ccc487720d4e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256467 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}-4(4)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)(t+1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/00/002f547b882940d087d6ed9d228334ec163931cfea0665eafdfe8b6ec8d863da.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255962 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1069 "q-120758" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1072 #uuid "b3d8130e-0be6-57fc-a522-c7397d721b9c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256468 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}+4(2)+C \\\\ 0 & =8-16+8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t=t(t-2)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/92/9254ea32fc55b2a703e52f55f4292d9708eef63ae147a9f1d2eac40b85eff319.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<2$ or $t>2.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256237 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1069 "q-120762" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1071 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1072 #uuid "b2a17c10-d59a-5539-82f1-c464939b55af" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256469 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256055 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1069 "q-120767" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1071 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1072 #uuid "3388ffa2-ee03-590b-be7c-ff4c9b15895e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256470 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x={\\theta }^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},$ where $\\theta \\in [0,360),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256239 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1069 "q-120771" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1071 "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1072 #uuid "dbeced00-09aa-5843-8bf0-6c51146e3b55" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256471 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/26/269d503189ab1027c3d8028542ef0c6a0e2f146aef0f7916691428866ce1b894.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256188 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1069 "q-120772" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1071 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1072 #uuid "14deb52a-082f-591b-aca6-b8367b0307e4" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256472 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255928 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1069 "q-120777" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1071 "What is the general solution to the equation $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1072 #uuid "6f6e626a-7316-545f-b95c-1ee669a112e6" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256473 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/85/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256286 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1069 "q-120782" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1072 #uuid "994feea1-4e21-534e-9a72-c1ddab9354dc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256474 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256260 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1069 "q-120789" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1071 "![](images/81/8197d1111bbd4c3b8908bc12cbc58561ffd23fe06d9f9fa99f9ab815ed78733f.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1072 #uuid "b6e12aa1-2e93-5d2f-b460-31a769deb627" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256475 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/ba/ba34bf96662682ec433c3ade0bf3f118638212101a0729fe95bbae05aee8bf1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256036 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1069 "q-120791" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1071 "![](images/c2/c27398934832fba39571fb74c9fa2f9ef80d5ff8ea27136f5353a67ea2cd9ae6.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1072 #uuid "96e27304-f86a-5e06-b358-08529dbd60ec" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256476 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c78678fade9fd2a60003a0ab2cac0299ef1e7df9f6633b1fc6a97422e13dfc4d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256310 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1069 "q-120806" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1072 #uuid "e823d758-1614-515f-a561-96579da144df" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256477 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1=({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use simplify our expression and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} & =({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2} \\\\  & =({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={tan}^{4}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1={tan}^{4}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256147 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1069 "q-120807" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1072 #uuid "93b7d709-aec4-5e7c-b981-7907596e3508" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256478 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}={cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256143 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1069 "q-120808" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1072 #uuid "38275387-16ad-5210-9a86-c85c7908724b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256479 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256018 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1069 "q-120811" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1072 #uuid "7513b531-411e-52b6-bdf7-4cddcd08fc38" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256480 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & =({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2}+2\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2} & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{4}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2}+2{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{4}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256313 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1069 "q-120817" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=16t-{t}^{3},$ where $t>0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1072 #uuid "c82954a1-ea49-5379-ae30-0facbbb1d022" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256481 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 16t-{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(16-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ t(4-t)(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =16-3{t}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{″}(4) & =16-3(4)^{2} \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(4)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (16t-{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=44.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+C & =44 \\\\ 32-4+C & =44\\,⟹\\,C=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=16+8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(4)=16+8(4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{4}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256149 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1069 "q-120828" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1071 "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1072 #uuid "722c6645-d9eb-5787-9276-c39131ce1533" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256482 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})=41.810{…}^{∘}≈{41.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${41.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc0c031fe6ccbb40dc77474582b301456f537ee2a649ba2d5b3bca9b1240618f.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{41.81}^{∘} \\\\  & ={138.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256307 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1069 "q-120831" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1072 #uuid "f839a0a0-2182-5a96-8bd7-233e711db5a4" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256483 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})=0.411\\,516…≈0.411\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.411\\,5$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.411\\,5.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.411\\,5.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.411\\,5.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab3de51a23485b1776e014768b0775315694cd1147eb2772f71323d4d097dee2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.411\\,5 \\\\  & ≈2.730\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈0.411\\,5\\cdot 2.730\\,1≈1.1,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256202 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1069 "q-120835" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1071 "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1072 #uuid "5d378f08-b29e-5a1d-8989-f385e5bd482e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256484 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=38.682{…}^{∘}≈{38.68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${38.68}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/12/12b17e3994be5e45af3aca185b22618dd0ec7288fba18a4131387783323247a0.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{38.68}^{∘} \\\\  & ={141.32}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256183 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1069 "q-120841" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1072 #uuid "63649519-348e-5199-9643-ece3096d2d11" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256485 1074 "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}$ | $\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256294 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1069 "q-120842" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1072 #uuid "5d0b02b3-02ea-5d58-9f01-5a623c01d6e0" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256486 1074 "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot e \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256067 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1069 "q-120843" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=30\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1072 #uuid "ede40a23-6b86-57f9-a4cb-1abc89e03e59" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256487 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(5)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-4(5)+C \\\\ 30 & =125-75-20+C \\\\ 30 & =30+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t & =0 \\\\ t({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =0 \\\\ t(t-4)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-1,0,4.$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard solutions $t=-1,0.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256198 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1069 "q-120847" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1072 #uuid "8eee8bd5-fde5-5053-8d96-174f236616df" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256488 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255984 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1069 "q-120856" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1071 "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-8$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1072 #uuid "c4999664-f9f8-55c5-b29e-56d4b2fd62ad" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256489 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-8 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{8}{9})=-62.733\\,955{…}^{∘}≈-{62.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{62.73}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{62.73}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/62/62808ce876d31757e1b46863b014bbf83209c414099ade0f93b91303c6702d1f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{62.73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={242.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘}={242.73}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{117.27}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256081 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1069 "q-120866" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1071 "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1072 #uuid "ea5f6930-5a56-5b62-afc3-a832751db214" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256490 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256200 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1069 "q-120871" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1071 "![](images/0a/0ac836789132a294051f6cbd0756f26407d002fa83a278b3b8825cfdd9af0b81.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{2}+3x-2$ and $y=x+1,$ shown above?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1072 #uuid "8032ade7-3d19-5c43-8480-bbb43c428ef2" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256491 1074 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/29/299608a54c0b5f568f32619722dd678af1388065f017620071ffb0473a686fa3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256270 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1069 "q-120887" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1072 #uuid "4bbca5fa-199a-5992-9918-2001be7e80c8" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256492 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $60\\cdot 300=18\\,000.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256258 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1069 "q-120893" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1072 #uuid "814abbe2-963b-55aa-99fd-84de01c3f66e" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256493 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](images/49/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256253 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1069 "q-120896" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1072 #uuid "70af0da8-cc0a-5a44-abbf-395198d56bd5" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256494 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{7}{9}) & =2.461\\,918…≈2.461\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.461\\,9$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.461\\,9.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.461\\,9 & ≈0.679\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.679\\,7.$\n\n![](images/ea/eafc9be7094c9c0ccdf167cc737d494efec40014a89ad793f7ed34ea5eed94ae.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.679\\,7 \\\\  & ≈3.821\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.461\\,9\\cdot 3.821\\,3≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255986 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1069 "q-120897" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1072 #uuid "f5b4de0b-e539-5073-8d28-b01b45e692e3" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256495 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈5.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255977 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1069 "q-1209" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1071 "Which statement is **not** true?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1072 #uuid "41287893-42e5-5645-847e-36b3acb93ca0" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256496 1074 "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆{Z}^{+}$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a positive integer. For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Z}^{+}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256327 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1069 "q-120909" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-x,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1072 #uuid "f43d71db-fe76-59ca-b0b6-16095e69fdb4" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256497 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=2-1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=3-2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+1=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-x=4-3=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈5.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255974 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1069 "q-120939" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1072 #uuid "5f5a5614-e888-59e5-9874-8aadaab8105b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256498 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=0+(-1)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y={1}^{2}+(-2)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256299 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1069 "q-120940" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1072 #uuid "4031e085-738d-5b9f-898b-4694f1a9c939" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256499 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256318 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1069 "q-120971" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x-2)(y+1)^{2}.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>-1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1072 #uuid "eb9cbf9f-dcf5-5005-a0a3-6bcc79a2f642" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256500 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a66a57932dbc935b5ed6f74b4f0faa0047c4b7ef58ded46be088dad30fa5eee4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/52/52efe97fcffa3da05151b3ffef5305e0c3ec0d567d3f62b964a30740c08a3981.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/aa/aa100568c18b31507c775e77ed3201a5aed6f708b541e48046617a43fe9a8304.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256348 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1069 "q-120981" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1072 #uuid "1acd4d45-a455-5e34-97cf-5720edb8c513" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256501 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](images/23/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256164 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1069 "q-120982" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.4$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1072 #uuid "9aeebf68-0741-58dc-8bdd-83268638886f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256502 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.4)=66.421{…}^{∘}≈{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${66.42}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d2b6dbe16a9537db672ddb3b369dbb49132a9f8860c774f3b8c79d478711f95.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{66.42}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=66.42\\cdot (-66.42)≈-4\\,410\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255975 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1069 "q-120986" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{2}+t-\\frac{3}{2}.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=4?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1072 #uuid "527a8b12-4f9f-5de5-a6a1-259595fcdf68" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256503 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{t}^{2}+t-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}t)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[2(4)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(4)]-[2(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)] \\\\  & =[128+8-6]-[2+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}] \\\\  & =130-1 \\\\  & =129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=4$ is $129.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256112 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1069 "q-120989" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.9$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1072 #uuid "abd67305-653a-5c08-b58a-02cfc97cf5bc" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256504 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.9)=25.841\\,932{…}^{∘}≈{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${25.84}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c65ecb4fdcefc332b75f2b459f65ba872a2d2d0826b27ba80df1c8f8f3b606e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{25.84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=25.84\\cdot (-25.84)≈-670\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255978 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1069 "q-120997" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1072 #uuid "1a5320c3-6f32-5efe-979e-244ce14b368d" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256505 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {45}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256139 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1069 "q-120998" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t+1}.$ At time $t=0.5$ the particle is located at the position $x={e}^{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1072 #uuid "3ad853df-f7ff-5b1c-95e8-898b94e2247f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256506 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=0.5$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0.5,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(0.5)+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & ={e}^{2}+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+1}]}_{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(2)+1}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(0.5)+1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256011 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1069 "q-121000" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=3{e}^{3t-1}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=2{e}^{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=5?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1072 #uuid "c4b2fe08-e151-518f-a268-0b785fdaa096" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256507 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5) & =2{e}^{2}+{\\int }_{1}^{5}3{e}^{3t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+{[{e}^{3t-1}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+({e}^{3(5)-1}-{e}^{3(1)-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+({e}^{14}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+{e}^{14}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+{e}^{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x={e}^{2}+{e}^{14}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255959 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1069 "q-121001" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1072 #uuid "879c6f82-eae3-56cf-9655-29a95317031b" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256508 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256223 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1069 "q-121005" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1071 "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1072 #uuid "2efb6d19-7876-5a5b-8775-75cbca9e7692" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256509 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256268 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1069 "q-121007" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ the particle is located at the position $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi ?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1072 #uuid "e663c698-6d0d-52a5-9b2e-c44b567d2f32" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256510 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256054 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1069 "q-121011" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1071 "![](images/18/18904539463b95f9d681f61f0d8f875ad0cb03b35d4037e75c227d4523502193.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=5?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1072 #uuid "3fcacee3-c17a-5da5-b61c-c5f1fb560ec6" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256511 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=5.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(5)=2+6=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=8.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255970 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1069 "q-121014" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1071 "![](images/cb/cb931a29b19302801d6586c37b4d2282e37776aa58b761f1fcb088a8fa19f9ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1072 #uuid "73e37777-c4bd-5ace-84de-06c7697ad5a6" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256512 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=1,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=1+4=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256039 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1069 "q-121021" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1071 "![](images/1d/1df00f421b20b101d1500b4e2f47054f0c79dcfd2d82585fb8fb3735adb71af5.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=5.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=9?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1072 #uuid "f6e05494-8289-5214-8dfa-f41f78ddc6c1" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256513 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=9$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,9].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(9)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=5,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=9.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(9)=5+2=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=9$ is $x=7.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256071 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1069 "q-121028" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi ,\\,x=a\\pi +n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1072 #uuid "98907701-34e4-57d9-9e78-7459fe40c307" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256514 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{7}{8}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256345 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1069 "q-121063" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1072 #uuid "48b98ebe-1777-506b-aeab-b7357f560c5c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256515 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\cdot {30}^{∘}+2\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={60}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256266 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1069 "q-121064" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1072 #uuid "7899ba3b-5e1d-54a1-a05f-4c185d5adaf5" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256516 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=-\\frac{1}{9}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256171 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1069 "q-121066" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1072 #uuid "c6a9809a-4ab2-58a8-8350-cebc2231a946" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256517 1074 "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=-\\frac{1}{18}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256006 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1069 "q-121068" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={480}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1072 #uuid "29983882-90b9-5637-81ac-662f4d53cf33" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256518 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}={120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}={240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x & ={480}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=240.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256083 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1069 "q-121071" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\\,x={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1072 #uuid "8b568634-0b04-5e19-bef7-4effff3320c7" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256519 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,\\frac{x}{3}={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘},\\,x={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot 1\\,{080}^{∘}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=360.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256367 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1069 "q-121076" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5x)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5},\\,x=a+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1072 #uuid "996f53f7-c88a-58b4-b0cd-6c032c212962" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256520 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n5x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,5x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n, & \\,5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5}, & \\,x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{30}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{7\\pi }{30}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256349 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1069 "q-121084" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5}=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1072 #uuid "e53f6ea2-bba9-5fc3-b077-9e3052adc57f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256521 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =5-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x=0$ is the only solution." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256121 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1069 "q-121088" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1071 "Solve the equation $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1072 #uuid "e1c3d7f6-ef8f-5d1f-858d-0177761d5470" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256522 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx={360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={0}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256178 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1069 "q-121119" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1071 "Solve the equation $7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3}=-5\\sqrt{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1072 #uuid "84fb9695-2616-55d3-bd8c-4022cb09276f" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256523 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3} & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-7\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi +(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi +(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256046 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1069 "q-121127" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=8$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1072 #uuid "c1252830-b58d-56d0-beaa-bb7162765f44" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256524 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)=82.874\\,983{…}^{∘}≈{82.87}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${82.87}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={82.87}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{82.87}^{∘}={262.87}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256092 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1069 "q-121130" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1071 "Solve the equation $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-33=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1072 #uuid "14f26350-13e7-52ce-aa23-842037776357" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256525 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-33=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =33 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext,  we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(11)=84.805\\,571{…}^{∘}≈{84.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${84.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={84.81}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{84.81}^{∘}={264.81}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={85}^{∘},{265}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256240 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1069 "q-1212" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊇B$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1072 #uuid "dc49fe00-4ce7-5ef1-a571-3c5c05755fd2" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256526 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256196 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1069 "q-12124" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1072 #uuid "6e3e3b1c-0354-523c-88ee-c0560a031e2c" 13194139533397 17592186045585 true] [17592186256527 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 